---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Teaching | Byron David Lencrerot III" description="Teaching interests and future academic direction of Byron David Lencrerot III.">
  <section class="m-hero plain" style="background-image:url('/images/chalkboard.svg')">
    <div class="m-in">
      <p class="kicker">05 / TEACHING</p>
      <h1>Helping Others<br />Learn and Grow</h1>
      <p class="lede">My goal is to develop lectures, videos, and learning resources that make technology understandable and practical for both children and adults.</p>
      <div class="m-actions"><a class="btn" href="#teaching-approach">Explore Teaching Content <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="inner-page">
    <div class="content-grid"><div class="side-label" id="teaching-approach">TEACHING APPROACH</div><div class="prose">
      <h2>Guided learning, followed by independent practice</h2>
      <p>One of the main influences on my developing teaching philosophy is Norman Eng’s book <em>Teaching College</em>. I especially connect with the I, We, You approach as a practical foundation for helping students move from seeing a concept demonstrated to practicing it with support and then applying it independently.</p>
      <p>I want students to understand the reasoning behind a solution, not simply memorize which buttons to click or which lines of code to copy. For technical subjects, that means making my thinking visible, working through examples together, and then giving learners a meaningful opportunity to try the skill themselves.</p>
      <p>My professional background spans hands-on telecommunications service and software development. As I prepare for a future in higher education, I want to connect those experiences to lessons that are structured, relevant, and accessible to students with different levels of technical experience.</p>
      <p>This page is a growing portfolio, not a claim that I currently hold a faculty appointment. It describes the principles I am developing and the learning resources I plan to create.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="page-rule"></div>
    <div class="content-grid"><div class="side-label">CORE INSTRUCTIONAL MODEL</div><div class="prose">
      <h2>I, We, You</h2>
      <p>I use this model as a foundation for thinking about how to teach a new skill. The goal is to gradually shift the work from instructor guidance to student independence.</p>
      <div class="card-grid teaching-model" style="margin-top:24px">
        <article class="info-card"><p class="kicker" style="margin-bottom:12px">01 / I DO</p><h3>I demonstrate</h3><p>I introduce the concept and model the process. I explain the decisions I am making, why I make them, and how I approach mistakes or unexpected results.</p></article>
        <article class="info-card"><p class="kicker" style="margin-bottom:12px">02 / WE DO</p><h3>We practice together</h3><p>We work through a similar example as a group. Students contribute ideas, answer questions, and help make decisions while I provide prompts, feedback, and support.</p></article>
        <article class="info-card"><p class="kicker" style="margin-bottom:12px">03 / YOU DO</p><h3>You apply it independently</h3><p>Students try the skill themselves in a new but related task. This gives them an opportunity to demonstrate understanding, discover where they need more practice, and build confidence without relying on a worked example.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">For a programming lesson, that could mean I demonstrate how to break a problem into steps, we design an algorithm together, and students then develop their own algorithm for a different problem. The level of guidance can change with the difficulty of the material and what students need.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="page-rule"></div>
    <div class="content-grid"><div class="side-label">HOW I WANT TO TEACH</div><div class="prose">
      <h2>More than a sequence of steps</h2>
      <p>The I, We, You structure is a starting point, not the entirety of my teaching philosophy. I also want my lessons to be purposeful, active, and connected to what students are learning and why it matters.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Make the thinking visible.</strong> Explain the reasoning behind a solution, not just the final answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Use concrete examples.</strong> Connect abstract concepts to familiar situations and realistic technical problems.</li>
        <li><strong>Check for understanding.</strong> Give students opportunities to explain their reasoning and reveal where they may be stuck.</li>
        <li><strong>Move toward independence.</strong> Provide enough structure to support learning, then let students apply the idea themselves.</li>
        <li><strong>Learn through practice.</strong> Treat mistakes as useful information and help students develop a repeatable problem-solving process.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These are the principles I want to carry into future lectures and learning materials. They reflect how I am interpreting and applying ideas from my reading, rather than claiming that every item is a direct quotation or named method from the book.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="page-rule"></div>
    <div class="content-grid"><div class="side-label">TEACHING TOPICS</div><div class="card-grid" style="margin-top:0">
      <article class="info-card"><h3>Technology made understandable for kids</h3><p>Introduce computational thinking through familiar activities, stories, visual examples, and small experiments that make abstract ideas concrete without talking down to young learners.</p></article>
      <article class="info-card"><h3>Coding concepts for adult learners</h3><p>Explain programming and software concepts clearly for beginners, career changers, and adults who want to understand not only how to write code, but why it works.</p></article>
      <article class="info-card"><h3>Software engineering in real applications</h3><p>Connect algorithms, APIs, databases, debugging, and application design to the kinds of systems people use and developers maintain.</p></article>
      <article class="info-card"><h3>LeetCode meets the real world</h3><p>Take the technique behind a coding challenge and show how similar thinking can help solve a business problem or support a feature in a real application.</p></article>
    </div></div>
    <div class="page-rule"></div>
    <div class="content-grid"><div class="side-label">VIDEO & LECTURE CONCEPTS</div><div class="prose">
      <h2>A connected collection, not just individual videos</h2>
      <p>I want to create content that can stand on its own while also fitting into broader lecture themes and learning series. Some videos will introduce foundational ideas for younger audiences; others will explain coding and software engineering to adults. A recurring thread is helping viewers connect a concept to something they can understand, practice, and use.</p>
      <div class="timeline">
        <article class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-date">LECTURE SERIES</div><div><h3>Coding concepts explained for different audiences</h3><p>Build connected lessons that explain one idea at a time, moving from an intuitive explanation to a worked example and then to how the concept appears in software. The explanation and examples can be adapted for children, adult beginners, or learners with more experience.</p></div></article>
        <article class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-date">REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</div><div><h3>From LeetCode challenge to business solution</h3><p>Start with a coding challenge, explain the problem-solving strategy and trade-offs, then translate the underlying idea into a realistic business problem or feature in an application. The goal is to show why the technique matters beyond passing a coding exercise.</p></div></article>
        <article class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-date">SOFTWARE ENGINEERING</div><div><h3>How concepts work inside real applications</h3><p>Explore how individual ideas connect to the larger system: how data moves, how an API supports a feature, how a database represents information, and how developers test and troubleshoot behavior.</p></div></article>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">These are planned content directions, not published lectures. As the material develops, each topic can link to a full lecture, a shorter video, code examples, and supporting notes.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="black-panel" style="margin-top:40px"><p class="kicker" style="color:#aeb3ba">BUILDING A TEACHING PORTFOLIO</p><h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;letter-spacing:-.04em">From clear explanations to practical application.</h2><p style="margin:0;line-height:1.8">As materials are developed, this page can grow into a connected library of lectures, short videos, demonstrations, code examples, and supporting notes for both younger and adult learners. Across those formats, I want to explain the concept, model the reasoning, practice it together, and then show how the idea can be applied independently to real software and business problems. The goal is not only to teach a solution, but to help learners recognize when and why to use the underlying idea.</p></div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .inner-page{width:min(1120px,calc(100% - 72px));margin:0 auto;padding:72px 0 100px;color:#191b1f}
  .kicker{font-size:.66rem;letter-spacing:.16em;font-weight:700;color:#737880;margin:0 0 22px}
  .page-heading{font-size:clamp(3rem,7vw,6.5rem);line-height:.94;letter-spacing:-.075em;font-weight:650;margin:0 0 28px;max-width:950px}
  .page-heading span{color:#8a8f96}
  .page-lede{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.8;color:#5e636b;max-width:760px;margin:0}
  .page-rule{height:1px;background:#d9dce0;margin:44px 0}
  .content-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:220px 1fr;gap:50px}
  .side-label{font-size:.65rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.13em;color:#7c8188}
  .prose h2{font-size:1.55rem;letter-spacing:-.04em;margin:0 0 15px}
  .prose p,.prose li{font-size:.93rem;line-height:1.9;color:#5d626a}
  .prose p{margin:0 0 17px}.prose ul{padding-left:21px;margin:0 0 25px}
  .info-card{border:1px solid #d9dce0;padding:24px;background:#fff}
  .info-card h3{margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.05rem;letter-spacing:-.02em}.info-card p{margin:0;color:#646971;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.8}
  .card-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:14px;margin-top:30px}
  .tag-row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-top:16px}.tag{border:1px solid #d5d8dc;padding:7px 10px;font-size:.67rem;font-weight:700;color:#5f646b}
  .black-panel{background:#111214;color:#fff;padding:28px}.black-panel p{color:#c2c5ca}
  .timeline{border-top:1px solid #d9dce0}.timeline-item{display:grid;grid-template-columns:160px 1fr;gap:28px;padding:24px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #d9dce0}.timeline-date{font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.08em;color:#777c83;font-weight:700}.timeline-item h3{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.2rem}.timeline-item p{margin:0;color:#626770;line-height:1.8;font-size:.87rem}
  .action-link{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin-top:22px;padding:13px 16px;background:#111214;color:#fff;text-decoration:none;font-size:.76rem;font-weight:700}.action-link:hover{background:#36393e}
  @media(max-width:700px){.inner-page{width:calc(100% - 36px);padding:48px 0 70px}.content-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:16px}.card-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.timeline-item{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:9px}.page-lede{font-size:.98rem}}

  .teaching-model{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))}
  .card-grid .info-card{min-height:190px;border-top:2px solid #111315}
  .timeline-item{background:rgba(255,255,255,.82);padding:25px 20px}
  .black-panel{margin-top:40px;border-left:5px solid #9aa1a8}

</style>
